  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "## Summary\n\nValtuutus 2.2 adds a MySQL and MariaDB provider and makes nested lookups and attribute reads much faster on Postgres and SQL Server.\n\n## Changes that need action\n\n- **Run the new migration** for your provider. Existing Postgres and SQL Server databases need  (see Databasemigrations in the README):\n  - Postgres adds  on . On a large table, consider running it as  outside a transaction.\n  - SQL Server rebuilds  to include the transaction columns.\n- **Postgres  default is now  (was ).** Its levels query now stops at the first level that adds nothing, so a high count costs nothing on shallow hierarchies, and a hierarchy up to twelve levels deep takes one round trip. Set it explicitly to keep the old behaviour. SQL Server stays at .\n\n## New\n\n- **MySQL and MariaDB provider** (, MySQL 8.4+ and MariaDB 10.11+) via  with  (#338).\n  - Batched check and lookup round trips, with the snap token resolved in the first round trip, matching Postgres' round-trip counts (#339).\n\n## Performance\n\n- **Postgres:** LookupSubject levels statements no longer re-plan on every call (#340). BenchmarkDotNet  269 → 115 µs.\n- **Postgres:** lookup levels walk a hierarchy with one bounded recursive query (#342).  −42%,  −25% on top of #340.\n- **SQL Server:** each lookup level is driven from the previous one, with a fixed join order and no parallelism (#343).\n  - Plans no longer depend on statistics: the same statement used to take 0.36-7 ms, and a deep userset chain up to 0.23 s on a large table.\n  -  −25..30%.\n- **Postgres and SQL Server:** attribute-wide reads (every entity of a type holding an attribute) seek their own rows (#344).\n  - A rare attribute on a large type: Postgres 11 ms → 34 µs, SQL Server 3.9 ms → 66 µs.\n- **SQL Server:** exclusion lookups (a negation's universe) read their id list once (#344). For users, 283 ms → 14 ms on the benchmark dataset.\n\n## Pull requests\n\n- #338 feat(mysql): add MySQL/MariaDB data provider\n- #339 feat(mysql): batch check and lookup round trips\n- #340 perf(postgres): plan LookupSubject levels statements generically\n- #342 perf(postgres): walk lookup levels with a bounded recursive query\n- #343 perf(sqlserver): drive each lookup level from the previous one\n- #344 perf(data): index attribute-wide reads and read exclusion lists once",
